---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseSection from "../../components/CaseSection.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import Glance from "../../components/Glance.astro";
import Stat from "../../components/Stat.astro";
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="sli-query-builder"
  team="PM, engineering manager, 5 engineers & a content designer"
>
  <Glance>
    <Fragment slot="challenge">
      Defining a Service Level Indicator (SLI) required advanced knowledge of
      query syntax, metrics, and attribute schemas. That technical prerequisite
      became an obstacle, leading to high abandonment in the SLI setup process.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="strategy">
      A context-aware query builder that surfaces only valid options, automates
      setup via templates, and warns before users save a broken SLI.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="impact">
      Weekly active accounts up 16% and weekly active users up 11% within 8
      weeks; contributed to 1,500+ beta users and $1.52M MRR at GA.
    </Fragment>
  </Glance>

  <CaseSection num="01" title="The challenge">
    <Prose>
      <h3>The complexity of setting up SLIs</h3>
      <p>
        Setting up a custom SLI in New Relic means filling NRQL queries into a
        couple of blank input fields — and to do that, users need to know the
        metric type, the right function, attribute names, and more. Some of the
        details users need to know when writing an SLI query:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Understanding the service levels practice and the logic of an SLI</li>
        <li>
          Knowing what you could measure for your data (e.g. latency, response
          time, throughput)
        </li>
        <li>Knowing their own telemetry schema and attribute names</li>
        <li>Deciding a threshold value that is meaningful for their service</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Think of it like building a filter for a spreadsheet you've never seen
        before. You know what you want to find, but you don't know the column
        names, the data format, or which operators to apply.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <Figure
      src="/images/sli-query-builder/v1-sli.png"
      caption="The original SLI setup — blank NRQL input fields"
    />
    <Prose>
      <p>
        <strong>Low task completion rate.</strong> From event tracking data, we
        saw users struggled to complete the SLI setup, with only an 11.1%
        completion rate. Another problem was users misconfiguring the good and
        valid events, which leads to a 100% SLI — users thought their services
        were healthy while in reality it was a misleading picture of their
        service health.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <Figure
      src="/images/sli-query-builder/funnel.png"
      caption="SLI setup funnel — an 11.1% completion rate"
    />
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="02" title="Discovery">
    <Prose>
      <h3>Our user group</h3>
      <p>
        Service level management was introduced by Google SRE teams and is now
        widely adopted across the industry. When designing the SLI query builder,
        we needed to address three key personas:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>SRE:</strong> automates infrastructure and resolves issues;
          establishes performance baselines across teams and services.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Developer:</strong> monitors service uptime, performance, and
          end-user experience.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Business leader:</strong> minimises costs from downtime and
          drives operational efficiency.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Understanding the pain points</h3>
      <p>
        I partnered with a UX researcher and PM to gather customer feedback
        through qualitative interviews. The research aimed to answer three key
        questions:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>What is blocking adoption?</li>
        <li>What problems do users encounter during SLI setup?</li>
        <li>Who is actually executing the setup flow?</li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
    <Figure
      src="/images/sli-query-builder/user-interview.png"
      caption="Qualitative user interviews"
    />
    <Prose>
      <h3>Research findings</h3>
      <p>The interviews surfaced three key insights:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>SLI/SLO practice:</strong> some teams are not fully aware of
          the benefit of service level management and don't know what would be a
          good metric to measure, especially with no dedicated SRE team.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ease of use:</strong> some customers are not certain about the
          attributes within their telemetry schema, so they cross-check a lot
          when using the query builder. Another common issue is unfamiliarity
          with NRQL syntax.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Human error:</strong> customers report SLIs being inaccurate or
          not working — which actually came from invalid good or bad event setup.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Known constraints</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          There is always going to be some translation gap between a customer's
          data and New Relic's data model — meaning there will be cases where
          customers can't find any metric useful to them in an SLI.
        </li>
        <li>
          Every SLI must be linked to an entity. For customers who prefer not to
          tie an SLI to a specific technical component, the work-around is to use
          a Workload as a logical container.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
    <Figure
      src="/images/sli-query-builder/research-notes.png"
      caption="Research synthesis — note-taking table"
    />
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="03" title="Design approach">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        A query is essentially a series of decisions. The design strategy is to
        surface those decisions one at a time, in the right order, with only
        valid options at each step.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Recognition over recall:</strong> instead of asking users to
          remember or copy/paste attributes within each clause, we offer
          dropdowns to lower the technical barrier. Users simply select from the
          available data points — taking away cognitive load and preventing
          human errors like typos.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Automation via smart templates:</strong> some SLIs are
          typically used to measure the performance of certain entity types
          (APM, Browser, database). By suggesting SLIs and thresholds based on
          historic data, we eliminate the setup hurdle and make it accessible for
          non-technical users. For power users, we still offer custom SLI setup.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Prevent errors before they happen:</strong> we show a warning
          and disable saving when we detect the same query for both good and
          valid events. This guardrail makes sure what users configure is
          actually meaningful, not just technically valid.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Simplify comparison by formatting:</strong> by changing the
          query into a line-by-line format, we improve scannability and make it
          easy to cross-check the valid versus the good/bad event.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
    <Figure
      src="/images/sli-query-builder/builder-1.png"
      caption="Recognition over recall — guided dropdowns"
    />
    <Figure
      src="/images/sli-query-builder/builder-2.png"
      caption="Automation via smart templates"
    />
    <Figure
      src="/images/sli-query-builder/builder-3.png"
      caption="Prevent errors before they happen"
    />
    <Figure
      src="/images/sli-query-builder/builder-4.png"
      caption="Line-by-line formatting for scannability"
    />
    <Figure
      src="/images/sli-query-builder/final.gif"
      caption="The improved SLI query builder in action"
    />
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="04" title="Business impact">
    <div class="grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <Stat
        value="+16%"
        label="Weekly active accounts, with weekly active users up 11% within 8 weeks."
      />
      <Stat
        value="$1.52M"
        label="MRR at GA, plus 1,500+ beta users — reducing SLI misconfiguration."
      />
      <Stat
        value="$2M CRR"
        label="Business impact from filling the gap in the service level creation flow."
      />
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="05" title="Looking back">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        The biggest UX risk in highly technical products is the silent failure.
        Shift the memory burden from the user to the system. By requesting data
        in incremental steps, we minimise the cognitive load required to recall
        critical information. Bridging the gap between specialists and laypeople
        demands a solution that is intuitive.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseStudy>
